.TH VITALS 1 "November 2026" "LRS Vitals 1.3.0" "LRS Services"
.SH NAME
vitals \- LRS Vitals, Linux server health monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vitals
[\fIcommand\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vitals
opens a full-screen interface (in the style of nmtui) with live graphs of CPU, memory, filesystems, mounts,
network, disks and processes, the health reports already written, and every setting.
The first time it runs it scans the server and asks a few questions, one page at a time.
.PP
It checks only what is present on the server. A rescan runs every Sunday at 02:00.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (none)
Open the health screen (the first-time setup runs first if it has not been done).
.TP
.B setup
Run the first-time setup again.
.TP
.B dashboard, reports, config, help
Open that screen directly.
.TP
.B run
Run a complete health check now and write the reports.
.TP
.B scan [--apply]
Show what is present on this server; with \fB--apply\fR, leave out what is not.
.TP
.B web [start|status|install|remove|passwd|useradd|userdel|users|cert|url]
The web interface.
.TP
.B schedule, watch, history, export, ...
All other \fBhealthcheck\fR commands work too; see \fBhealthcheck --help\fR.
.SH KEYS
Up/Down move, Enter opens or edits, Space switches yes/no, F10 saves, Esc goes back.
Where a connection does not send arrow keys or Esc: Ctrl-N/Ctrl-P or j/k move, Ctrl-B or q goes back.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/lshme/*.conf
Settings (mode 600).
.TP
.I /var/lib/lshme
Reports, history and the last server scan.
.TP
.I /var/log/lshme
Logs and the audit log.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR healthcheck (1), systemhealth (1)
.SH AUTHOR
LRS Services Pvt Ltd, Mumbai.
